WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:23.066
[Music]

00:00:23.066 --> 00:00:28.980
[Narrator 1:] From the very beginning, the human race has had to contend with problems of survival, disease, and death.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:40.460
Through the ages, entire populations were decimated and became extinct, leaving behind only scant remnants of their cultures.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.960
It's difficult to establish at what point in history medicine appeared,

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:56.720
but we have some record of diseases being treated in various parts of the world as far back as 2000 years BC.

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:02.300
[Narrator 2:] Throughout the centuries, navigation was developed.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:09.020
With the movement of people around the planet, terrifying epidemics of cholera, yellow fever, and plague began to spread.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.400
Nations gradually realized that there was a need to fight these diseases together,

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:19.740
and in 1851 the first International Health Conference was held in Paris.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:33.480
In 1919, a new strain of influenza struck more victims in a few months than had been killed throughout the first World War.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.520
The League of Nations did consider health issues,

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:45.380
but it was only after the Second World War that the United Nations conference drew up the plan for a separate international health body.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:53.960
[Narrator 1:] In September 1947, when cholera broke out in Egypt, the World Health Organization sprang into action.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:02:00.460
It couldn’t afford to wait for its formal constitution which was signed on April 7th, 1948,

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:07.060
the day since celebrated globally as World Health Day.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:15.020
In June 1948, the first World Health Assembly opened in Geneva with 53 countries represented.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:21.660
[Dr Brock Chisholm:] The suffering of millions of human beings in scores of countries will be alleviated,

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:24.960
and many, many thousands of lives will be saved.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:29.540
[Clapping]

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.920
[Narrator 2:] Quarantine regulations were drawn up to curb the spread of communicable diseases.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:37.900
But the root causes of ill health also had to be tackled.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:45.980
[Dr M.G. Candau:] The only enemies worth fighting are ill health, poverty, and ignorance.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:53.160
[Narrator 2:] Malaria, a widespread fatal disease, was one of the top priorities facing the new organization.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:03:00.720
In the early 1950s, spectacular successes were achieved through systematic spraying with DDT.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:06.580
In 1955, fearful that the mosquitos would develop resistance to the spraying,

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:11.700
the World Health Organization launched a global campaign to eradicate malaria.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:21.460
The dream was not to be. By the late '60s, it was clear eradication was not possible.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:25.100
Controlling malaria was a more realistic approach.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:31.740
Smallpox: One out of four people it touched died.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.920
The rest were marked for life. Millions were affected.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:43.400
In 1958, countries started massive vaccination campaigns to wipe out the disease worldwide,

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:49.240
but to vaccinate every man, woman, and child on earth proved an overwhelming task.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:56.820
In 1967, the World Health Organization launched a new campaign with a different approach:

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.700
Contain the disease at each outbreak. Stop the chain of transmission.

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:07.700
Every case had to be tracked down. All who had been in contact had to be found and vaccinated.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.740
The search went on everywhere, in schools, public places, through person-to-person contacts.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:18.960
Even financial rewards were offered for those reporting cases.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:24.500
The question was asked over and over again:  Have you seen anyone who looks like this?

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:34.440
Step by step, the disease was pushed back to the very last case:

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:36.760
Ali Maow Maalin.

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:46.600
With Ali, a train of transmission as old as mankind was broken. Smallpox was no more.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:53.660
On the 8th of May 1980, Dr. Halfdan Mahler, Director General of the World Health Organization,

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:55.940
signed a certificate of eradication.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:05:00.940
The ten-year campaign cost three hundred million dollars, a real bargain.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:06.940
The world saves each year one thousand million dollars on vaccines and care of the sick alone.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:12.880
The saving in human suffering has no price.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:18.233
Important as the elimination of smallpox was,

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:24.560
hundreds of millions in the less-developed countries were still trapped in a vicious cycle of poverty and disease.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:28.840
Most people had no access to basic health care.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:32.760
Average life expectancy hovered at fifty.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:40.960
Many mothers died in childbirth and many children like these died before reaching adolescence.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.060
[Narrator 1:] Something had to be done.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:49.260
A new effective long-term plan that would benefit everyone.

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:56.020
Health for all by the year 2000 was the goal set at the World Health Assembly in 1977.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.660
A year later, the way ahead was declared at Alma-Ata.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:08.660
In villages such as this one, a new vision began to take root,

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:11.020
a vision based on primary health care.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:16.680
That is, health for people in their homes, schools, villages where they live and work.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:21.900
A vision based on local men and women trained to care for common health problems.

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:34.080
[Narrator 1:] Forty years ago, the World Health Organization was created to help build a healthier world.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:40.160
Where are we today? World population has reached the five billion mark.

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:44.120
Remarkable progress has been made in science and medicine,

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:47.900
yet the health picture varies widely.

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:56.300
In industrialized nations, many millions are invested in medical technology, but blatant inequalities in health status remain.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:08.040
[Narrator 2:] High technology takes us ahead. It also takes its toll.

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:12.120
For example, nuclear accidents pose lasting threats to health.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:21.860
[Music]

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:29.400
In response to these dangers, groups are demanding control over their health, and individuals are taking personal action.

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:36.560
In the poorer nations, the picture is very different.

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:42.580
Three quarters of the world's population receive less than ten percent of the world's budget for health.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:49.240
Women have a two hundred times greater risk of dying during pregnancy and childbirth than women in the rich nations.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:56.420
[Narrator 1:] While these countries still face the diseases of poverty, they must learn to confront the diseases of affluence.

00:07:57.380 --> 00:08:02.220
Fueled by the adoption of western lifestyles, alcohol and tobacco use surge upward.

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:05.080
So do heart disease and cancer rates.

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:12.300
For the first time in their history, these countries must battle yesterday's and tomorrow's diseases at the same time.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:22.280
Today, the world is facing a grave new threat: the AIDS virus.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:28.440
We don’t know where is came from, but we do know that it brings suffering and death.

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:34.520
More than half of the people diagnosed with AIDS have died.

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:40.860
Fortunately, it can only be spread from one person to the next in a few ways:

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.420
sex, blood, and from mother to child.

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:50.200
The AIDS virus is already present on every continent.

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:54.240
Governments acting individually cannot stop its spread.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:59.300
The only way it can be stopped in any country is for it to be stopped in all countries.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:07.180
From WHO the call has now gone out to the family of nations, to nations, and to individuals.

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:10.620
The call that says that we know how to stop AIDS,

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:18.540
and what is now required is that responsible actions take place at the individual, the national, and the international level.

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:23.120
[Narrator 2:] Over the past forty years,

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:28.680
the World Health Organization has shown what can be achieved through international cooperation.

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:36.360
Eradication of smallpox, a major reduction in child mortality through immunization and anti-diarrheal disease control,

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:40.860
a confidence to promote safe motherhood programs

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:45.040
which aim to halve maternal deaths in developing countries by the end of the century.

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:49.620
Control of tuberculosis and leprosy through treatment at home,

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:54.840
and many other similar low cost solutions too numerous to mention.

00:09:55.420 --> 00:10:01.340
These gains, the millions of lives saved, and the untold suffering avoided have been achieved

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:08.320
through involving people in their own health and development, cooperation between countries,

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:11.040
research, and proper use of appropriate technology.

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:14.320
Strengthening health systems and health information,

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:19.100
that is, through WHO's primary health care approach.

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:27.800
[Music]

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:34.200
[Dr Halfdan Mahler:] Over the past forty years, we have learned the difficulty

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:39.920
in WHO that development, and that includes of course health development,

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:43.100
cannot be achieved by proxy.

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:49.060
It has to be carried out by the people concerned themselves.

00:10:50.300 --> 00:10:53.460
But to help them do that in a self-relying way,

00:10:53.820 --> 00:11:00.760
WHO has shown them how much they themselves can do to improve their own health.

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:04.400
And so it has promoted research and development,

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:14.460
leading to a string of low-cost technologies that can be adapted to different social, economic, and cultural situations,

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:18.480
and afforded even by the poorest countries.

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:27.980
But most important, WHO has shown how to set up the permanent structures required in the health and many other sectors

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:35.920
to make sure that technology becomes accessible to the masses and not just to the privileged few.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:42.480
The Alma-Ata Declaration on Primary Health Care, now ten years old,

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:48.660
clearly mapped out the road on which we are firmly engaged.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:53.640
People everywhere from north to south, east to west,

00:11:53.980 --> 00:12:03.320
recognize beyond all their differences that health is good for all people and essential indeed for human progress.

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:08.440
That there’s both economic value and social justice in health.

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:15.420
Surely we must all recognize that even if health is not everything,

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:19.260
there is nothing without health.

00:12:19.660 --> 00:12:29.160
In the interest of the human race, there must health for all and all for health.

00:12:29.960 --> 00:13:00.420
[Music]

